$DIR/main.p:49:10: chain cannot return the closures of this call: the call reaches chain again, so its region would have no fixed size
    A function that returns a closure builds it in a region its caller provides,
    and the region holds the regions of the calls whose closures it returns in
    turn (spec section 4.5). chain returns closures that hold those of this
    call, and the call can reach chain again, directly, through other functions,
    or through a func value or an interface, so its region would have to hold
    itself.
    Build the closures in a loop instead of by recursion, or keep their state in
    a value the caller owns, such as a struct with a method.
    Go allocates each closure on the heap, however deep the recursion. Plutonium
    builds closures in frames, whose sizes are fixed when compiling.
    (see "Closures do not outlive their frame" in the Plutonium spec, section
    4.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3102" for a longer explanation)

